<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gurgen I of Lori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 979–<a href="/years/989">989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Kiurike I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gurgen, also known as Kiurike I, was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-iii-of-armenia">Ashot III of Bagratid Armenia</a> and founder of the Kingdom of Lori, also called Tashir-Dzoraget, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Around 979 he received the northern provinces and took the title of king, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiurikian-dynasty">Kiurikian dynasty</a>. The kingdom's monasteries of Haghpat and Sanahin flourished under his line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gurgen-i-of-lori.jpg" alt="Gurgen I of Lori" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yerevantsi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haghpat_monastery_bas_relief.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gurgen, who also bore the name Derenik, was the younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashot-iii-of-armenia">Ashot III the Merciful</a> and Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrovanuysh">Khosrovanuysh</a>. Kyurike was the local pronunciation of his name, and from it the dynasty and the kingdom took the name Kyurikian. From the beginning of the 10th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratid-armenia">Bagratid Armenia</a> had been fragmenting, as powerful local princes sought independence and local kingdoms and principalities emerged that only formally acknowledged the Bagratids of Ani. Tashir had been run by governors from outside the royal house until Ashot III, needing reliable control of this strategic region, made his eldest son Smbat its governor.</p><p>When Smbat became king as Smbat II, he appointed Gurgen governor of Tashir in his place. In 974 Gurgen took part in the campaign Ashot III organised in response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> expedition of the Byzantine Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The kingdom of Tashir-Dzoraget grew out of this governorship gradually, and the date of its proclamation is disputed, with proposals ranging from 966 to 988; Mkhitar Ayrivanetsi is the only early writer to give a year. A khachkar of 986 in the cemetery of Kurthan village already gives Gurgen, as Kyurike, the royal title. He recognised the supremacy of his elder brother and held his share of the inheritance on condition of submission. A decree of Smbat II, issued at Sanahin in 979, describes the lands entrusted to him, from the valleys of the Pambak and Aghstev rivers to the vicinity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>, bordering the Bagratid Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Tiflis, the Pahlavunis and the Shaddadids.</p><p>By the same decree the Holy Mother of God Church of Sanahin became the cathedral of a new diocese under Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaiah">Isaiah</a> covering Gurgen's lands. Gurgen continued the building of the monastic complexes of Sanahin and Haghpat begun by his parents, and gave two large chandeliers to Sanahin. In 988 he joined Smbat II's campaign against King Smbat of Abkhazia, supporting David Kuropalates and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-iii-of-georgia">Bagrat III of Georgia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Accounts of his end differ. One says that Gurgen, a deeply devout man, abdicated of his own will and spent eight years as a monk at Sanahin, where he died; another that he died in 989, the year of his brother Smbat II's death. He was buried in the Holy Mother of God Church of Sanahin, where a plain triangular tombstone is inscribed as the resting place of Gurgen, son of Ashot the Merciful. His elder son David Anhoghin succeeded him, with the younger son Smbat as a co-ruler who left the government to his brother. The historian Aristakes Lastivertsi mentions a daughter who married Apusuarin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dvin">Dvin</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kiurike I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 979 <b>Gurgen</b>, son of Ashot III of Bagratid Armenia, was given the northern provinces of Tashir and Dzoraget and took the title king, founding the Kyurikian line, named after his Georgian-style name Kyurike.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lori">Kingdom of Lori</a></li></ul></section>
